---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ArticleHero from '../../components/ArticleHero.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Dry Hair Care Guide: Rebuilding Moisture"
  description="How to care for dry hair, reduce breakage, and rebuild moisture over time."
  canonicalPath="/hair-care/dry-hair/"
>
  <ArticleHero eyebrow="Hair Care" title="Dry Hair Care" image="/images/gallery/gallery-esthetics-room-1.jpg" imageAlt="Private treatment room" />
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-14">
    <p class="text-xs text-ink/50 mb-2"><a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a> / <a href="/hair-care/" class="hover:underline">Hair Care</a> / Dry Hair</p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Dry hair often results from a combination of heat styling, chemical
      processing, sun exposure, and low humidity — usually not one single cause but
      several small factors stacking up over months. Recovery is usually gradual
      rather than instant, since damaged hair can't be fully repaired, only managed
      and grown out. This guide covers what actually helps.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">What Causes Hair to Feel Dry</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Dryness typically comes down to the hair's outer cuticle layer being rougher
      or more open than it should be, which lets moisture escape and prevents hair
      from reflecting light smoothly (which is part of why dry hair often looks
      dull as well as feeling rough). Heat tools, chemical lightening, frequent
      washing with harsh shampoos, and sun or chlorine exposure are the most common
      contributors, and they tend to compound each other.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Practical Steps</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-ink/80 mb-6">
      <li>Reduce heat styling frequency and always use a heat protectant when you do use hot tools.</li>
      <li>Add a weekly deep conditioning treatment, focused on mid-lengths and ends rather than the scalp.</li>
      <li>Trim regularly to remove damaged, split ends before they travel further up the hair shaft.</li>
      <li>Switch to a sulfate-free shampoo, which cleanses without stripping as much natural oil.</li>
      <li>Limit how often you fully shampoo if your hair tolerates it — over-washing can worsen dryness for some hair types.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Protecting Hair From Further Damage</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      If you're actively trying to recover from dryness, it helps to pause anything
      that's actively contributing — that might mean spacing out color
      appointments, lowering the heat setting on styling tools, or wearing a hat or
      UV-protectant spray during long sun exposure. Recovery and continued damage
      happening at the same rate tend to cancel each other out, so reducing new
      damage matters as much as adding moisture back in.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Realistic Expectations</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      It's worth being clear-eyed about what products and treatments can and can't
      do: they can meaningfully improve how dry hair looks and feels, and they can
      help prevent further damage, but they can't reverse structural damage that's
      already happened to the hair shaft. The most reliable way to fully remove
      damaged hair is, eventually, a trim — everything else is management in the
      meantime.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">When to See a Professional</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      If dryness comes with noticeable scalp irritation, flaking, or hair thinning
      beyond what feels normal, consult a dermatologist rather than relying on
      cosmetic products alone — those symptoms can have causes that a salon
      treatment isn't equipped to address, and a professional evaluation is the
      more reliable next step.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Building a Recovery Timeline</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      It helps to set realistic expectations from the start: noticeably improving
      dry hair usually takes a few weeks of consistent care to feel a difference,
      and several months before the visibly damaged portion has fully grown out
      or been trimmed away. Think of it less as a fix and more as a shift in
      routine that you maintain going forward — the habits that got hair back to
      a healthy baseline are generally the same ones that keep it there, so it's
      worth treating this as a permanent adjustment rather than a temporary push.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Environmental Factors Worth Managing</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Indoor heating and air conditioning both reduce ambient humidity, which can
      quietly dry out hair even without any change to your product routine. A
      humidifier in the room where you sleep, particularly during winter months,
      is a small environmental adjustment that can meaningfully reduce how dry
      hair feels day to day — something worth trying before assuming a product
      change is needed.
    </p>

    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      See also: <a href="/hair-services/hair-treatments/" class="text-rose-600 underline">Hair Treatments</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-3">Consistency Over Intensity</h2>
    <p class="text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      A gentle routine followed consistently tends to outperform an aggressive
      one used sporadically. Small, sustainable changes to your everyday habits
      generally beat an occasional intensive treatment used as a quick fix.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
